  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1015 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nWednesday through Monday:\n\nHigh pressure tracks east into the Great Lakes region Wednesday,\nwith a light northeast breeze forcing a stalled frontal zone\nacross IL to sag further south. Mostly cloudy skies may linger,\nbut predominantly dry and pleasant weather is expected across \nsouthern WI Wednesday with highs in the 60s. \n\nMeanwhile twin upper troughs deepen to our west. One over the\nRocky Mountains will seed low pressure development over the\ncentral Great Plains, and another over south-central Canada will\nform a clipper system. The Great Plains low will follow the\naforementioned frontal zone into IL, with rainfall from that\nsystem reaching our region on Thursday and continuing into\nThursday night (60-80% chances). The low should pass near the\nsouthern tip of Lake MI Thursday evening, continuing northeastward\nand gradually merging with the aforementioned clipper system into\nFriday. Some isolated embedded thunderstorms remain a possibility\nduring the rainfall Thursday into Thursday night, but no strong or\nsevere storms are expected at this time given the lack of entry\ninto the warm sector. The departing low pressure drags a cold\nfront across southern WI Thursday evening, turning winds northwest\nand gradually sweeping away any lingering precip late Thursday\nnight. \n\nThe two low pressure systems (and their upper troughs) should\nphase together into one unified system, further amplifying the \nwave train of the polar jet. The departing low pressure (now \nintensifying rapidly) will work with strong high pressure \ndescending from Canada into the Plains to form a strong pressure \ngradient over WI, with strong NW winds (gusting to 30-40 mph) \narriving late Thursday night and continuing into Friday afternoon.\nConfidence in these gusty winds continues to increase as ECMWF \nand GEFS Ensembles converge on the phasing / behavior of this \nsystem.\n\nHigh pressure will then slowly track eastward into WI/IL Friday \nnight. Winds are forecast to rapidly subside late Friday, with\nskies clearing out. As such, we have high potential for frost \n(and possibly even a localized freeze) Friday night into early \nSaturday morning, with lows in the 30s. After a daytime high in \nthe 50s Saturday, lows return to the 30s Saturday night into \nSunday morning, though likely a few degrees warmer than the \nprior night due to some southerly WAA as high pressure continues\neast from the Ohio River Valley. A gradual warming trend then \nensues into early next week, returning daytime highs to the 60s.\n\nSheppard",
